"quinupristin-dalfopristin":{rl:"IV streptogramin combination dosed 7.5 mg/kg every 12 hours over 60 minutes per label, mixed and flushed with dextrose 5% only; phlebitis, arthralgia, myalgia and CYP3A4 interactions are the key issues.",rh:"Severe infusion site pain or thrombophlebitis, disabling joint or muscle pain, signs of allergy, frequent diarrhea, or rising bilirubin typically prompt holding and notifying the provider.",ml:"Streptogramin B plus A (30:70) 50S ribosome inhibitor labeled for complicated skin and skin structure infections from MSSA or S. pyogenes; historically used for VRE faecium.",mp:"Active against vancomycin-resistant E. faecium but not E. faecalis; inhibits CYP3A4 (cyclosporine levels rise); saline and heparin are incompatible."},
"ethionamide":{rl:"Oral second-line TB drug, 15 to 20 mg/kg/day (maximum 1 g per day) per label, taken with other TB drugs and pyridoxine; GI upset, hepatotoxicity, neuropathy and hypothyroidism are the key risks.",rh:"Jaundice or dark urine, visual changes, numbness, depression or psychotic symptoms, or hypoglycemia in diabetes typically prompt holding and notifying the provider.",ml:"Thioamide prodrug activated by EthA that inhibits InhA; labeled for active TB resistant to isoniazid or rifampin or with intolerance to other drugs, in combination regimens.",mp:"inhA promoter mutations confer cross-resistance with isoniazid; the thioamide structure explains hypothyroidism, especially combined with para-aminosalicylic acid."}
