"dalbavancin": "Dalbavancin suggests a recent single-dose IV treatment for a skin infection, sometimes in people who inject drugs. The drug persists for about two weeks, so delayed allergic reactions remain possible.",
"fosfomycin": "Fosfomycin suggests a recent single-dose treatment for a bladder infection. Fever, flank pain or vomiting afterward can point to a kidney infection or urosepsis, which a single dose does not cover.",
"methenamine": "Methenamine suggests recurrent urinary tract infections, often in older women. It only prevents infection in the bladder, so fever, confusion or flank pain can still reflect an active UTI or urosepsis.",
"trimethoprim": "Trimethoprim suggests a urinary infection or UTI prevention. It is linked to hyperkalemia, especially with ACE inhibitors, ARBs or spironolactone in older adults, which can present with weakness or bradycardia.",
"nitazoxanide": "Nitazoxanide suggests diarrhea from Giardia or Cryptosporidium, often after daycare, pool or travel exposure. Dehydration is the main concern, and bright yellow urine from the drug is harmless.",
